(6. Viva questions and answers \(1-9\)) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 762.5199212598424765 Td
(Short answers are a starting point. Add the relevant example when answering.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 737.0081102362204319 Td
(Q. What is the aim of BasisBridge?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 721.4175590551180903 Td
(A. To transform a vector successively through three valid bases, display intermediate coordinates and matrices, and recover) Tj
T* (the original vector through reverse transformations.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 683.4333070866141497 Td
(Q. What is a basis?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 667.8427559055118081 Td
(A. A linearly independent set of vectors that spans the vector space. Every vector in the space has a unique coordinate) Tj
T* (representation in that basis.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 629.8585039370078675 Td
(Q. What is the dimension of a vector space?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 614.2679527559055259 Td
(A. The number of vectors in any basis of the space. R^2 has dimension 2; R^3 has dimension 3.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 589.6065354330708033 Td
(Q. How are basis vectors stored in this project?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 574.0159842519684616 Td
(A. As the columns of a square basis matrix. This matches the equation v = Bc for the coordinate column c.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 549.3545669291338527 Td
(Q. What is the difference between a vector and its coordinate column?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 533.7640157480313974 Td
(A. The vector is the geometric object; its coordinate column is a list of coefficients relative to a chosen basis. The same) Tj
T* (vector can have different coordinate columns.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 495.7797637795275136 Td
(Q. How do you convert standard coordinates into basis B?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 480.1892125984251152 Td
(A. Solve v = Bc. Since B is invertible, c = B^-1v, which the app writes as [v]_B = B^-1v.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 455.5277952755904494 Td
(Q. What is the change-of-basis matrix from B1 to B2?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 439.9372440944881077 Td
(A. P21 = B2^-1 B1. It converts coordinates in B1 into coordinates in B2: [v]_B2 = P21 [v]_B1.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 415.2758267716534988 Td
(Q. Why is the destination basis inverted?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 399.6852755905511003 Td
(A. We first turn the source coordinates into the geometric vector using B_source, then express that vector using the) Tj
T* (destination basis by multiplying by B_destination^-1.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.11 0.165 0.247 rg
51.0236220472441033 361.7010236220471597 Td
(Q. Do coordinates change the geometric vector?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.357 0.424 0.506 rg
51.0236220472441033 346.1104724409448181 Td
(A. No. Under a pure change of basis the geometric vector is unchanged; only the coefficients used to describe it change.) Tj
ET
